["er.eventlog.import-anchor/1",{"authority":{"logical_scope":"aep","stream_identity":"01a0d3bf-067c-7068-af65-bd964ce78fcb","tenant":"planning"},"completeness":"available_evidence_only","evidence":[],"instance":{"entity":"story","fields":{"body":"## Context\n\nEvery write verb commits. `new`, `move`, `relate`, `body` and `set` each take effect the moment they\nexit, and a search of `crates/*/src` and `crates/*/tests` finds no `--dry-run` on any of them.\n\nThis is what makes a bulk write expensive to be wrong about. The `story-migration` skill in\n`beyond10x/agentplugins` exists partly to work around it: its first two steps produce the migration\nplan as a document and stop, so a person can read what twenty creates would do before any of them\nruns. That is a dry run implemented in prose because the CLI has none.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `new`, `move`, `relate`, `body` and `set` accept `--dry-run`.\n- With it, each prints exactly what it would print on success, writes no file, and appends nothing\n  to `journal.jsonl`.\n- A refusal is still a refusal under `--dry-run`: a `move` the ladder does not allow exits 1 and\n  names the legal rungs, because the value of the flag is finding that out without a write.\n- A test asserts the journal is byte-identical before and after a `--dry-run` sweep.\n\n## Evidence for the gap\n\n`crates/edge/aep-cli/src/planning.rs` — no verb declares the flag. The nearest existing precedent\nfor verify-then-commit is `reverse init` (`reverse.rs`), which verifies its protocol source before\nwriting `project.yaml` and cleans up only the directory it created.\n","document":{"events":[],"fields":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":0,"id":"ent-779eef2f526ee474abc34f5c2a91437b","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/dry-run-on-the-write-verbs","provenance":{"created_by":"system","updated_by":"system"},"revision":7,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":0}},"body":"## Context\n\nEvery write verb commits. `new`, `move`, `relate`, `body` and `set` each take effect the moment they\nexit, and a search of `crates/*/src` and `crates/*/tests` finds no `--dry-run` on any of them.\n\nThis is what makes a bulk write expensive to be wrong about. The `story-migration` skill in\n`beyond10x/agentplugins` exists partly to work around it: its first two steps produce the migration\nplan as a document and stop, so a person can read what twenty creates would do before any of them\nruns. That is a dry run implemented in prose because the CLI has none.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `new`, `move`, `relate`, `body` and `set` accept `--dry-run`.\n- With it, each prints exactly what it would print on success, writes no file, and appends nothing\n  to `journal.jsonl`.\n- A refusal is still a refusal under `--dry-run`: a `move` the ladder does not allow exits 1 and\n  names the legal rungs, because the value of the flag is finding that out without a write.\n- A test asserts the journal is byte-identical before and after a `--dry-run` sweep.\n\n## Evidence for the gap\n\n`crates/edge/aep-cli/src/planning.rs` — no verb declares the flag. The nearest existing precedent\nfor verify-then-commit is `reverse init` (`reverse.rs`), which verifies its protocol source before\nwriting `project.yaml` and cleans up only the directory it created.\n","scope":[{"confidence":"cited","path":"crates/edge/aep-cli/src/planning.rs"},{"confidence":"cited","path":"crates/edge/aep-cli/src/reverse.rs"}],"status":"draft","summary":"No verb has --dry-run, so the only way to find out what a bulk write does is to do it.","title":"A write verb can say what it would do without doing it"},"lifecycle_state":"draft"},"scope":[{"confidence":"cited","path":"crates/edge/aep-cli/src/planning.rs"},{"confidence":"cited","path":"crates/edge/aep-cli/src/reverse.rs"}],"summary":"No verb has --dry-run, so the only way to find out what a bulk write does is to do it.","title":"A write verb can say what it would do without doing it"},"id":"ent-779eef2f526ee474abc34f5c2a91437b","lifecycle_state":"draft","revision":7,"version":1},"order":"per_kind_only","subject":["story","ent-779eef2f526ee474abc34f5c2a91437b"]}]